// Buff self-host port of `crates/buff-lang-ast/src/lib.rs` (P4.4).
//
// Buff AST crate — abstract syntax tree node definitions.
//
// This crate is **pure data**: it defines the node types produced by parsing
// and consumed by later stages (type checking, codegen). It contains no
// parsing logic and no type checking.
//
// # Module layout
//
// - op: binary/unary operator enums (BinaryOp, UnaryOp).
// - common: Ident, Block, Param.
// - ty: TypeRef type references.
// - expr: Literal, Expr, MatchArm, Pattern.
// - stmt: Stmt.
// - decl: Decl and the specific declaration structs.
// - ir: the dataflow-graph intermediate representation
//   (IrGraph, IrNode, AstLowerer).
// - lossless: trivia-preserving source representation for LSP and
//   comment-preserving `buff fmt` (T57). See LosslessTree.
//
// Span is re-exported from `buff-lang-error` for convenience.
//
// Faithful 1:1 translation of the Rust reference. The Rust `lib.rs` is pure
// module wiring (`pub mod ...` + `pub use <mod>::*` glob re-exports + a single
// re-export of `Span` from `buff-lang-error`). Buff has no `mod` keyword and
// no `pub use` glob — the M7 monolith loader (Phase 5) concatenates sibling
// `.buff` files in the same directory into a flat namespace, and `export`
// controls visibility per symbol. There is therefore nothing executable to
// translate here; this file is a documentation-only header that mirrors the
// Rust crate-doc comment block and records the translation decision.
//
// Translation decisions:
//   * `pub mod common; pub mod decl; ...` (Rust module declarations) ->
//     DELETED. Buff has no `mod` keyword; files in the same directory are
//     auto-linkeded once the M7 monolith loader is active. Until then,
//     single-file `buff check` treats cross-file references as opaque
//     forward references (per `self-host/ast/stmt.buff` header lines 46-50).
//   * `pub use common::*; pub use decl::*; ...` (glob re-exports) ->
//     DELETED. Buff has no glob re-export. Each symbol is referred to by its
//     bare name from any sibling file in the same directory (the monolith
//     loader concatenates them into one namespace).
//   * `pub use buff_lang_error::Span;` (re-export of a foreign type) ->
//     DELETED. Buff has no syntax for cross-crate re-export; callers `import
//     { Span } from "../error/span"` directly when needed. The semantic
//     intent (Span is the canonical span type, defined in the error crate,
//     used everywhere in the AST) is preserved as a comment below.
//   * `pub use common::span_to_json;` (re-export of a free function) ->
//     DELETED for the same reason. Callers reach `span_to_json` via
//     `import { span_to_json } from "common"` once the monolith loader lands.
//
// The canonical span type used by every AST node.
//
// Defined in `crates/buff-lang-error/src/span.rs` (Rust) and ported to
// `self-host/error/span.buff` (Buff). Every node in this crate carries a
// `span: Span` field so diagnostics can point back at the source.
